Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "feature_steering_rare_region_design",
"Title": "Steering Off the Mode: Can Interpretable Features Guide Generative Protein Models Into Rare-but-Valid Design Space?",
"Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable SAE features act as directional handles that let protein language models reach LOW-likelihood-but-still-valid regions of sequence space (rare folds/motifs/property combinations) that likelihood-guided sampling systematically undersamples\u2014and they do so more efficiently and with higher validity than raw activation steering or temperature/top-p sampling. We further hypothesize that a feature's causal steering strength, not its concept-correlation, predicts how far off-mode it can push generation while retaining validity.",
"Related Work": "Existing SAE-and-steering work for protein LMs (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; 'Steering Protein Language Models'/ASPO 2025; the antibody SAE paper 2025; ProtoMech cross-layer transcoders 2026; Matryoshka SAEs on ESM2-3B 2025) all evaluate whether steering INCREASES a single target property near the model's high-likelihood modes. A recent review (Guiding Generative Models for Protein Design, 2025) explicitly flags that generative models undersample low-probability regions that often encode valuable properties, but proposes no interpretability-based remedy. SAE-Targeted Steering (Chalnev et al. 2024) improves side-effect control in text LMs but does not study distributional escape or rare-region reachability. Our proposal is the first to reframe interpretable-feature steering as a controlled DISTRIBUTION-ESCAPE tool for scientific design: quantifying how far off-mode features can drive valid generation, comparing against non-interpretable exploration baselines, and testing what feature property predicts safe off-mode reach. This turns interpretability from a descriptive add-on into a diversity-and-novelty engine.",
"Abstract": "Generative protein models sample most heavily from the high-likelihood modes of their training distribution, systematically neglecting rare regions of sequence space that may encode novel, valuable designs. Prior interpretability work shows that sparse-autoencoder (SAE) features of protein language models can steer generation toward single target properties, but always tests gains near the model's comfortable modes. We ask a different question: can interpretable features be used as controllable handles to push generation OFF the mode\u2014into low-likelihood but still-valid sequence space\u2014more efficiently and safely than non-interpretable exploration methods? We train SAEs on ESM-2 residual streams and, for each of a bank of property-linked features, sweep steering strength while tracking two quantities jointly: distributional escape (drop in sequence likelihood / distance from natural-sequence manifold) and validity (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, property-predictor agreement). We define a Reach-Validity Frontier and compare feature steering against raw difference-of-means activation steering, high-temperature/top-p sampling, and random-direction perturbation. We hypothesize that (i) interpretable features achieve a strictly better Reach-Validity frontier than non-directional exploration, (ii) they unlock rare property COMBINATIONS absent from natural training data while remaining foldable, and (iii) a feature's measured causal effect magnitude\u2014not its concept-correlation\u2014predicts how far off-mode it can safely reach. Results would establish interpretable features as an efficient, controllable engine for exploring novel scientific design space, and provide a mechanistic account of which features are trustworthy exploration directions.",
"Experiments": "1) SETUP: ESM-2 (150M and 650M) with TopK SAEs trained on the residual stream at a layer chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic; also test a Matryoshka SAE for robustness. Assemble a feature bank (~30-50 features) mapped to measurable properties: alpha-helix/beta-sheet content, GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, thermostability proxy (fine-tuned head), and 2-3 motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad). Automated evaluators: ESMFold pLDDT (foldability/validity), DSSP for secondary structure, biopython for GRAVY/MW, regex/HMMER for motifs, a light thermostability probe.\n2) DEFINE METRICS: (a) Off-mode reach = negative log-likelihood of the generated sequence under ESM-2 relative to unsteered baseline, plus distance to the nearest natural neighbor (via embedding kNN in a held-out UniRef sample). (b) Validity = ESMFold pLDDT and pseudo-perplexity thresholds and property-predictor confidence. (c) Reach-Validity Frontier = for each method, the Pareto curve of off-mode reach vs fraction of generations still valid.\n3) CORE COMPARISON: For each feature, sweep steering strength and generate N sequences per strength. Build the Reach-Validity Frontier for: (i) SAE-feature steering, (ii) raw difference-of-means activation steering, (iii) high-temperature and top-p sampling (non-directional distribution flattening), (iv) random-direction residual perturbation matched in norm. Test whether interpretable-feature steering dominates the frontier (achieves higher reach at equal validity), using paired statistics across features and seeds.\n4) RARE-COMBINATION UNLOCK: Identify property pairs that are anti-correlated or absent in natural sequences (e.g., very high beta-sheet + high hydrophilicity, or two motifs rarely co-occurring). Steer toward these rare combinations with features and with baselines; measure success rate of generating valid, foldable sequences hitting the rare target, and their novelty (max identity to any natural sequence, embedding distance).\n5) PREDICTOR OF SAFE REACH: For each feature compute (i) concept-correlation/interpretability score, (ii) causal effect magnitude (property change per unit steer at the origin), (iii) decoder-direction norm/orthogonality to the natural-sequence manifold, (iv) monosemanticity. Regress each feature's frontier area (safe off-mode reach) on these predictors; test whether causal magnitude beats concept-correlation (R^2 comparison, ablation).\n6) GENERALIZATION: Reduced protocol on an autoregressive protein/antibody LM (p-IgGen) with Ordered SAE features, and optionally a small-molecule SMILES LM, to test whether the Reach-Validity dominance of interpretable features transfers across object types.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Reach-Validity frontier area (primary), rare-combination valid success rate, novelty (identity/embedding distance), ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, diversity, and predictor R^2 for safe reach.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico validity (ESMFold pLDDT, predictors) is a proxy for wet-lab function; claims are about controllable exploration of model-defined valid space, not experimental viability. 2) 'Off-mode' reach measured by model likelihood may conflate genuinely novel-but-valid sequences with degenerate artifacts; we mitigate via multiple independent validity signals and novelty checks against natural databases. 3) High-temperature baselines may be tuned suboptimally; we sweep them thoroughly to give the strongest fair comparison. 4) The predictor-regression may be underpowered with a modest feature bank; mitigated by using tens of features across property types and multiple seeds. 5) Frontier dominance could be model/SAE-specific; mitigated by testing two model sizes, two SAE architectures, and a cross-domain object type."
}}
