Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "steerability_predictor",
  "Title": "Decodable but Inert: A Predictive Theory of Which Interpretable Directions Actually Steer Scientific Generative Models",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Across biomolecular generative models, whether an interpretable/linearly-decodable direction is causally steerable is NOT random noise but is predicted by cheap, intervention-free geometric quantities computed at the intervention site. We hypothesize a specific predictor - the alignment between the direction and the model's local output Jacobian (i.e., how much the downstream computation actually reads that direction) - forecasts steering efficacy far better than concept-correlation or probe accuracy, enabling a pre-flight test that saves expensive generation-based screening.",
  "Related Work": "Multiple recent papers independently report the same anomaly: high feature-concept correlation or linear decodability does not guarantee causal steering. The antibody SAE paper (2025) finds TopK SAE features are interpretable but not steerable. The Boltz-1 paper (Jedryszek et al. 2026) finds a beta-strand direction with F1=0.82 that produces zero causal effect. FEGA (Hoang et al. 2026) shows most SAE features lack a stable steering direction and distinguishes value-like vs pointer-like features by their logit-effect geometry. Each treats this as an obstacle or a descriptive taxonomy, and none provides a cheap PREDICTIVE test of steerability nor validates such a predictor as a screening tool across scientific object types. Prior steering work (InterPLM, ProtSAE, FoldSAE, ProtoMech, Matryoshka-SAE) demonstrates steering succeeds for SOME directions but selects them by trial-and-error generation. Our proposal turns the shared anomaly into a positive, testable theory: we define candidate intervention-free predictors of steerability, benchmark them against ground-truth steering across models and domains, and demonstrate a Jacobian-alignment 'steerability score' that predicts which directions steer BEFORE any generation. This is a mechanistic advance (why steering fails) and a practical tool (a pre-flight filter), distinct from all prior descriptive or single-direction steering work.",
  "Abstract": "A striking anomaly recurs across mechanistic-interpretability studies of scientific generative models: directions that are highly interpretable and linearly decodable frequently produce no causal effect when used to steer generation. Antibody, protein, and structure-prediction studies each independently report this decodable-but-inert phenomenon, yet none explains what distinguishes steerable from inert directions or offers a way to predict steerability before running expensive generation experiments. We propose a predictive theory. Our central hypothesis is that steerability is governed by how strongly the model's downstream computation actually reads a direction at the intervention site, which is captured by the alignment between the direction and the local output Jacobian (the sensitivity of generated properties to activation perturbations). We define a small set of cheap, intervention-free candidate predictors - Jacobian alignment, decoder-direction norm relative to the residual stream, effective read-out via attention/MLP weight projections, and value-like vs pointer-like effect geometry - and benchmark them against ground-truth steering efficacy measured by property change per unit steer. We evaluate across ESM-2 protein LMs, an antibody LM (p-IgGen), and a SMILES molecular LM, using SAE features and difference-of-means directions. We test whether Jacobian alignment predicts steerability with high rank correlation while concept-correlation and probe accuracy do not, and we validate a resulting 'steerability score' as a pre-flight filter that selects effective steering directions without generation, quantifying compute saved. Results would convert a widespread nuisance into an actionable mechanistic principle and a practical screening tool for controllable scientific design.",
  "Experiments": "1) GROUND-TRUTH STEERING BENCHMARK: For each model (ESM-2 150M/650M; p-IgGen antibody LM; a small SMILES molecular LM such as a GPT-style ChemLM), assemble a bank of 40-80 candidate directions per model that are all strongly decodable/interpretable: (a) SAE features (TopK and, where available, Ordered/Matryoshka) with high concept-correlation, and (b) difference-of-means directions for measurable properties. Properties with cheap automated evaluators: for proteins - alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex); for antibodies - CDR length, charge, developability proxies; for molecules - logP, QED, ring count, presence of functional groups (RDKit). For each direction, sweep steering strength and measure ground-truth steerability = slope of target-property change per unit steer at low strength, plus max reliable property gain before validity collapses (validity via pseudo-perplexity/ESMFold pLDDT for proteins, RDKit-validity for molecules).\n2) CANDIDATE PREDICTORS (all intervention-free, no generation): For each direction compute (i) concept-correlation / probe AUROC (baseline predictor), (ii) Jacobian alignment = norm of the projection of the property-Jacobian (gradient of a property predictor's output w.r.t. residual-stream activations, averaged over a held-out set) onto the direction, (iii) effective read-out = projection of the direction through downstream attention/MLP input weight matrices, (iv) decoder-direction norm relative to typical residual-stream norm, (v) value-like vs pointer-like classification from FEGA-style logit-effect geometry (dimensionality of the effect cloud under ablation across contexts).\n3) CORE TEST: Regress ground-truth steerability on each predictor. Primary metric: Spearman rank correlation between each predictor and steerability, with bootstrap CIs. Hypothesis-confirming result: Jacobian alignment (and/or effective read-out) achieves substantially higher rank correlation than concept-correlation/probe accuracy. Report per-model and pooled results; test whether concept-correlation is near-zero predictive, replicating the anomaly.\n4) PRE-FLIGHT FILTER VALIDATION: Treat the best cheap predictor as a 'steerability score'. Rank all candidate directions by the score, then measure precision@k for actually-steerable directions vs random and vs concept-correlation ranking. Quantify compute saved = generation runs avoided to find k good steering directions, relative to trial-and-error selection.\n5) MECHANISTIC ABLATION: Test causality of the Jacobian-alignment account by (a) constructing synthetic directions with matched decodability but deliberately low/high Jacobian alignment (via orthogonalizing a decodable direction against the top Jacobian subspace) and confirming steering efficacy tracks alignment, and (b) varying intervention layer to check the predictor tracks the layer-dependence of steerability.\n6) CROSS-DOMAIN TRANSFER: Report whether the predictor's utility holds across all three object types (protein, antibody, molecule), establishing generality of the principle.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Spearman rank correlation (predictor vs steerability), precision@k of the steerability filter, compute saved, R^2 of multivariate predictor, and synthetic-direction ablation effect sizes.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Jacobian alignment requires a differentiable property signal; for properties only measurable by non-differentiable evaluators (HMMER motifs, folding) we substitute a lightweight differentiable surrogate predictor, which may introduce approximation error - we quantify surrogate fidelity. 2) The predictor may be property- or layer-specific rather than universal; we mitigate by testing multiple properties, layers, and three model families and reporting where it fails. 3) In-silico steerability and validity are proxies for wet-lab outcomes; claims concern model-internal controllability, not experimental function. 4) The synthetic-direction ablation assumes orthogonalization cleanly removes Jacobian alignment without side effects; we verify by re-measuring alignment post-construction. 5) If steerability turns out to be predicted equally well by concept-correlation in some models, the anomaly may be architecture-dependent - a finding we would still report as scientifically informative."
}}
