Response Content: {"total": 4, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "616f7ab41a3f6c1542acc29e7d13ced79c640711", "title": "Mol-CADiff: Causality-Aware Autoregressive Diffusion for Molecule Generation", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2025, "citationCount": 0, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05499, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the sour
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "intervention_leak_scientific_design",
  "Title": "Does Clamping Fix It? Auditing Whether Interpretable-Feature Interventions Truly Eliminate Undesired Properties in Scientific Generative Models",
  "Short Hypothesis": "When we clamp/ablate an interpretable feature to REMOVE an undesired property (a toxic substructure, an instability motif, a disallowed fold) from a scientific generative model, the model often reconstitutes that property through alternative internal routes during iterative/autoregressive decoding. We hypothesize that feature-level suppression frequently 'leaks': the property re-emerges in the final object at rates that concept-correlation and single-forward-pass evidence would deem impossible, and this leakage is predictable from the redundancy (number of alternative encoding features) of the target concept.",
  "Related Work": "Almost all interpretability-for-design work (InterPLM, ProtSAE, FoldSAE, molecular-transformer SAE circuits, GFlowNet interpretation, chemical-LM steering vector fields, molecular task arithmetic) demonstrates that clamping/steering a feature CHANGES a target property, and reports success at the level of a forward pass or a single generation. A concurrent text-LLM paper (Cui et al. 2026, 'SAE Interventions are Unreliable: Post-Intervention Recovery') shows clamping an 'unsafe' feature in language models can be optimized around, revealing that suppression blocks one visible route but not the behavior. No prior work asks whether feature interventions ROBUSTLY eliminate undesired properties in SCIENTIFIC generative models, where 'recovery' is an objectively measurable structural fact (a toxic SMILES substructure appears; a destabilizing motif is present; a forbidden secondary structure forms) rather than fuzzy text behavior, and where the multi-step, structured decoding process (autoregressive SMILES, iterative diffusion denoising) provides many natural re-routing opportunities. We are the first to (i) define and measure intervention leakage for scientific object design, (ii) test whether it arises spontaneously during ordinary generation (not just adversarial optimization), and (iii) tie leakage to concept redundancy, yielding a practical audit + a redundancy-aware multi-feature clamping fix.",
  "Abstract": "Interpretability-guided design increasingly relies on a hopeful assumption: that clamping or ablating an interpretable feature reliably removes the property it encodes. We stress-test this assumption for scientific generative models, where 'the property' is an objectively verifiable structural fact\u2014a toxic substructure in a molecule, a destabilizing motif in a protein, or a disallowed fold. We define intervention leakage as the rate at which a suppressed, undesirable property reappears in the final generated object despite an active feature-level intervention. Using autoregressive SMILES chemical language models and ESM-2 protein language models with sparse autoencoders, we clamp features that a forward-pass analysis identifies as encoding a target undesired property, then run ordinary iterative generation and check the finished object with hard structural evaluators (RDKit substructure matching, HMMER/regex motifs, DSSP/ESMFold). We hypothesize that leakage is substantial and, crucially, arises during normal decoding rather than only under adversarial optimization, because the model can re-encode the concept through alternative features at later positions/denoising steps. We further hypothesize that leakage is predicted by concept redundancy\u2014the number of SAE features (or the effective rank of the concept subspace) that encode the target property\u2014and we validate a redundancy-aware fix that clamps the full concept subspace rather than a single feature. We report leakage rates, a redundancy-based predictor of intervention reliability, and the validity cost of subspace clamping. Results turn feature interventions from an assumed-safe knob into an auditable, quantifiable one, with direct implications for safe and reliable scientific design.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS & TARGETS: (a) An autoregressive SMILES chemical language model (a GPT-style ChemLM trained on ChEMBL/ZINC, or a public checkpoint) with a TopK SAE trained on the residual stream at a mid layer. (b) ESM-2 (150M/650M) with an SAE on the residual stream. Choose UNDESIRED properties with hard, non-model evaluators: for molecules\u2014presence of specific toxicophore/PAINS substructures, a reactive group, or exceeding a ring/heteroatom count (RDKit SMARTS matching); for proteins\u2014a specific catalytic/binding motif (HMMER/regex) and a secondary-structure target (DSSP on ESMFold).\n2) FEATURE SELECTION: For each target property, identify the SAE feature(s) that best encode it via concept-correlation and probe AUROC on held-out objects; record the SINGLE best feature and the RANKED set of features above a correlation threshold (the candidate concept subspace).\n3) LEAKAGE MEASUREMENT (core): Generate N objects (e.g., N=2000) conditioned to naturally exhibit the target property (via prompting/seed contexts or property-biased sampling). Apply the intervention\u2014clamp the best feature to a strong suppressive value at EVERY decoding step (every token for SMILES; every position for masked/iterative protein generation)\u2014and let generation complete normally. Measure Leakage Rate = fraction of FINISHED objects that still contain the target property according to the hard evaluator, despite the active clamp. Compare against: (i) no intervention (baseline occurrence), (ii) a single-forward-pass 'success' estimate (does the clamp lower the property logit/probe at the intervention step?), to expose the gap between forward-pass appearance of success and end-object reality.\n4) WHERE DOES IT LEAK? Trace, for leaked objects, WHEN the property re-emerges (which token/position/denoising step) and WHICH alternative features carry high concept-probe activation at that step, testing the re-routing hypothesis. Ablate/clamp those alternative features one-by-one and re-measure leakage to confirm they are the leak paths.\n5) REDUNDANCY PREDICTOR: For each target property compute redundancy metrics\u2014number of features above the correlation threshold, effective rank of the concept subspace (via SVD of per-object activation vectors of correlated features), and concept-probe accuracy retained after removing the top feature. Regress per-property Leakage Rate on these redundancy metrics; test the hypothesis that redundancy predicts leakage (Spearman correlation, bootstrap CIs) while single-feature concept-correlation does not.\n6) REDUNDANCY-AWARE FIX: Clamp the FULL concept subspace (project out the top-k correlated feature directions / the SVD concept subspace) instead of a single feature. Re-measure Leakage Rate as a function of subspace dimension k, and the VALIDITY COST (RDKit validity / pseudo-perplexity / ESMFold pLDDT and off-target property drift). Report the leakage\u2013validity frontier and the minimal k needed to drive leakage below a target threshold.\n7) GENERALIZATION: Repeat the reduced protocol on a masked/diffusion generation setting (e.g., a masked diffusion protein or SMILES model) to test whether iterative denoising leaks differently than autoregressive decoding.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Leakage Rate (primary), forward-pass-success vs end-object-reality gap, redundancy-predictor Spearman/R^2, leak-position distribution, leakage reduction vs subspace dimension, and validity cost (RDKit validity, pseudo-perplexity, ESMFold pLDDT, off-target drift).",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Hard evaluators define the property crisply, but choosing properties with unambiguous structural evaluators limits generality to such properties; fuzzy properties (e.g., 'developability') are out of scope. 2) Leakage could be an artifact of weak clamping strength; we mitigate by sweeping clamp magnitude up to the point of validity collapse and reporting leakage across the whole range. 3) SAE quality and layer choice may confound which features 'encode' the property; we control with multiple SAE architectures/layers and probe-based verification. 4) In-silico validity and property calls (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal controllability and structural facts, not experimental function. 5) The redundancy predictor could be underpowered with few target properties; we mitigate by including many properties per domain (tens of toxicophores/motifs). 6) If leakage turns out to be negligible, that is still a scientifically valuable positive result establishing intervention reliability, and the redundancy analysis explains why."
}}
