Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "interpretable_feature_bayesopt", "Title": "Optimizing in Concept Space: Do Interpretable SAE Features Make Scientific Design Optimization More Sample-Efficient and Auditable?", "Short Hypothesis": "Running Bayesian optimization directly over the sparse, monosemantic feature space of a sparse autoencoder trained on a frozen scientific foundation model yields more sample-efficient design than BO over the model's raw embeddings or a VAE latent space, AND provides human-readable design rationales. We further hypothesize that the per-dimension monosemanticity of the search space (not merely its dimensionality) is what predicts optimization efficiency, and that interpretability enables concept-prior warm-starting that further cuts oracle calls.", "Related Work": "Latent-space Bayesian optimization (LSBO) is a mature line: Automatic Chemical Design (Gomez-Bombarelli 2018), Constrained BO in VAE latent space (Griffiths & Hernandez-Lobato 2019), LaMBO (Stanton 2022), CLaSMO (2024), and low-dimensional semi-supervised latent BO for antimicrobial peptides (2025). Separately, SAE-steering work (InterPLM 2024, ProtSAE 2025, 'SAEs for Low-N Protein Function Prediction and Design' 2025, Steering Protein LMs/ASPO 2025) shows SAE latents can steer generation and even design high-fitness variants via manual latent steering. The crucial gap: (i) no prior work uses the INTERPRETABLE SAE feature space of a FROZEN foundation model as the actual optimization search space for closed-loop, oracle-guided Bayesian optimization; existing LSBO uses black-box VAE/embedding spaces, and the low-N SAE paper uses manual/greedy steering, not query-efficient BO with uncertainty. (ii) No one has tested whether the MONOSEMANTICITY of the search space is causally responsible for optimization efficiency, versus mere dimensionality reduction. (iii) No one exploits interpretability to warm-start BO with concept-property priors or to emit human-readable design trajectories. Our proposal isolates the value of interpretability as a search-space property, distinguishing it from generic dimensionality reduction, and from all prior LSBO and SAE-steering work.", "Abstract": "Scientific design\u2014of proteins, molecules, and sequences\u2014is a query-efficiency problem: each oracle evaluation is expensive, so the geometry of the search space matters. Latent-space Bayesian optimization (LSBO) searches the black-box latent of a task-specific VAE, while recent interpretability work shows sparse autoencoders (SAEs) decompose frozen foundation-model activations into monosemantic, concept-aligned features. We unify these threads and ask: does optimizing directly in an interpretable SAE feature space improve sample efficiency and auditability compared to optimizing in raw embeddings or a VAE latent? We build a closed-loop pipeline where a Gaussian-process surrogate proposes points in the SAE feature space of a frozen ESM-2 (proteins) or a SMILES chemical language model, decodes them back to residual-stream activations, and generates candidate objects scored by cheap in-silico oracles. We compare against embedding-space BO, VAE-latent LSBO (LaMBO-style), and genetic-algorithm baselines under matched oracle budgets. Our central scientific test is whether per-dimension monosemanticity, not just dimensionality, drives efficiency: we construct search spaces with matched dimension but varied monosemanticity (SAE features vs. random rotations vs. PCA) and correlate monosemanticity with optimization regret. We further exploit interpretability two ways: concept-prior warm-starting (initializing the GP mean from known feature-property associations) and human-readable design trajectories (reporting which named concepts each optimization step turned up or down). We expect interpretable-feature BO to match or beat baselines in regret-at-budget while additionally yielding auditable, controllable design paths\u2014turning interpretability from a descriptive luxury into a concrete optimization asset.", "Experiments": "1) PIPELINE SETUP: For proteins, use frozen ESM-2 (150M/650M) with a TopK SAE trained on the residual stream at a layer chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic; for molecules, a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM with a residual-stream SAE. Encoding: object -> foundation-model activations -> SAE feature vector (mean-pooled or per-position summary). Decoding: SAE feature vector -> reconstructed activations -> generate object (masked infilling / activation-conditioned decoding for proteins; activation steering during autoregressive decoding for SMILES). Verify round-trip fidelity before optimization.\n2) ORACLES (cheap, automated): proteins\u2014ESMFold pLDDT (validity), DSSP secondary-structure targets, GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, a fine-tuned thermostability/fitness head, and standard FLIP/ProteinGym-style fitness landscapes where available; molecules\u2014RDKit logP, QED, penalized-logP, docking score against a fixed target, synthetic-accessibility. Validity gates: RDKit-validity, pseudo-perplexity, pLDDT thresholds.\n3) CORE BO COMPARISON: Implement a GP+Expected-Improvement (and qEI for batches) optimizer over four search spaces under identical oracle budgets: (a) SAE feature space (ours), (b) raw foundation-model embedding space (dimensionality-reduced to match via PCA to isolate space identity), (c) VAE latent space (train a task-VAE, LaMBO-style baseline), (d) discrete genetic algorithm on the object directly. Primary metric: best-oracle-value vs. number of oracle calls (regret-at-budget), averaged over >=20 seeds and multiple target properties. Also report validity retention along the trajectory and novelty/diversity of found optima.\n4) MONOSEMANTICITY-CAUSES-EFFICIENCY TEST (the central scientific claim): Construct search spaces of MATCHED dimensionality but varied monosemanticity\u2014(i) SAE features (high monosemanticity), (ii) random-rotation of the SAE feature basis (destroys per-axis meaning, preserves geometry/dimension), (iii) PCA components of activations (dense, mixed), (iv) partially-trained/entangled SAE. Measure a monosemanticity score per space (mean per-feature concept-correlation / activation sparsity) and regress optimization efficiency (area-over-regret-curve) on monosemanticity across spaces and target properties. Hypothesis: monosemanticity predicts efficiency even at fixed dimension.\n5) CONCEPT-PRIOR WARM-STARTING: For target properties with known feature-property associations (from concept-correlation analysis), initialize the GP prior mean / restrict the initial design region using those associations. Measure oracle-calls-to-threshold vs. cold-start BO; quantify the warm-start speedup and test whether it holds only in the interpretable space (baseline spaces cannot be warm-started this way).\n6) AUDITABILITY DELIVERABLE: For each optimization run, emit a human-readable trajectory: at each accepted step, report the top named concepts increased/decreased (via LLM-generated or annotation-based feature labels) and the resulting property change. Evaluate faithfulness by ablating the reported concepts and checking the property effect matches; report a faithfulness score. This is a qualitative + quantitative differentiator absent in all baselines.\n7) GENERALIZATION: Run the reduced protocol across both domains (protein, molecule) and two model sizes to test robustness.\nEVALUATION METRICS: regret-at-budget / oracle-calls-to-threshold (primary), validity retention, novelty/diversity of optima, monosemanticity-vs-efficiency Spearman/R^2, warm-start speedup, and trajectory-faithfulness score.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico oracles are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern sample-efficiency and auditability of optimization, not experimental validation. 2) SAE encode/decode round-trip fidelity may be lossy, capping achievable optima; we quantify reconstruction quality and report its correlation with performance, and use the best available SAE architecture. 3) The interpretable space could win merely by favorable dimensionality; the matched-dimension monosemanticity experiment (step 4) is specifically designed to rule this out. 4) GP surrogates scale poorly to very high feature dimensions; we mitigate with sparsity-aware feature selection (optimize only over active/relevant features) and additive/trust-region GPs, reporting sensitivity to this choice. 5) Concept-prior warm-starting depends on the quality of feature-property labels, which may be noisy; we report warm-start gains with and without label filtering. 6) If interpretable-feature BO merely ties baselines on regret, the auditability and warm-start results still constitute a meaningful, publishable contribution establishing interpretability as a 'free' optimization asset."}}
