Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "cross_model_steering_transfer",
"Title": "Steer Small, Design Big: Transferring Validated Interpretable Steering Directions Across Scientific Generative Models",
"Short Hypothesis": "Because SAE feature spaces are approximately universal across models (similar under rotation-invariant alignment), a steering direction that is discovered and causally VALIDATED on a small, cheap generative model can be aligned and transferred to steer a larger or different-architecture model to produce objects with the same target property\u2014without re-running expensive steerability screening on the big model. We further hypothesize that transferred directions inherit not just the target effect but also the small model's causal steerability, so cheap models can serve as steerability-screening proxies for expensive ones.",
"Related Work": "Two lines converge here. (1) SAE steering of scientific generative models (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; Steering PLMs/ASPO 2025; ProtoMech cross-layer transcoders 2026; SAEs for low-N design 2025) all discover and validate steering directions SEPARATELY per model, requiring costly per-model SAE training and generation-based screening. (2) Feature-space universality work (Quantifying Feature Space Universality Across LLMs via SAEs, 2024) shows SAE feature subspaces are similar across text LLMs under rotation-invariant transforms and conjectures\u2014but never demonstrates for generation\u2014that steering vectors might transfer. No prior work (a) tests whether interpretable steering directions transfer BETWEEN scientific generative models to control the properties of GENERATED objects, (b) exploits a cheap model as a steerability-screening proxy for an expensive one, or (c) quantifies the design-efficiency savings of not re-discovering steerable features per model. The concurrent DiMaS (2026) finding that features are 'linearly decodable but not linearly steerable' makes the transfer-of-steerability question especially non-trivial and worth testing. Our proposal turns universality from a descriptive observation into a practical cross-model design tool.",
"Abstract": "Steering interpretable features to control generated proteins, molecules, and other scientific objects currently requires expensive, model-specific pipelines: train a sparse autoencoder (SAE), screen thousands of features for which ones actually steer, and validate on the target model. As foundation models grow, this per-model cost dominates. We ask whether this cost can be paid once on a small model and amortized: given evidence that SAE feature spaces are approximately universal across models, can a steering direction discovered and causally validated on a small, cheap generative model be aligned and transferred to steer a larger or different-architecture model? We build a cross-model transfer pipeline: (1) discover and causally validate steering directions on a small model (e.g., ESM-2 35M) using cheap in-silico property oracles; (2) learn a rotation/linear alignment between the small and large models' representation spaces from paired activations on a shared held-out corpus; (3) map the validated direction into the large model and steer generation. We evaluate on protein LMs across sizes (ESM-2 35M\u2192150M\u2192650M) and across architectures (masked ESM-2 \u2194 autoregressive protein LM), plus a molecular SMILES LM pair. Metrics: property-gain transfer efficiency (fraction of native steering effect recovered), validity retention (ESMFold pLDDT, RDKit validity, pseudo-perplexity), and\u2014crucially\u2014whether a direction's steerability on the small model predicts its steerability on the large one, enabling cheap steerability screening. Success would let practitioners screen and validate design controls on inexpensive models and deploy them on state-of-the-art ones, cutting the compute cost of controllable scientific design.",
"Experiments": "1) MODELS & ORACLES: Protein: ESM-2 at 35M, 150M, 650M (same family, different size) and an autoregressive protein LM (e.g., ProtGPT2 or p-IgGen) for cross-architecture transfer. Molecules: two SMILES LMs of different sizes/tokenizers. Cheap automated oracles define measurable target properties: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, thermostability proxy (light fine-tuned head), motifs (HMMER/regex, e.g., zinc finger); molecules\u2014RDKit logP, QED, ring count, functional-group presence. Validity: ESMFold pLDDT and pseudo-perplexity for proteins, RDKit-validity for molecules.\n2) SOURCE-MODEL DISCOVERY: Train TopK SAEs on the small model's residual stream (layer via intrinsic-dimension heuristic). For each property, identify candidate directions (SAE features + difference-of-means) and CAUSALLY VALIDATE by sweeping steering strength and measuring property gain vs. validity budget. Record each direction's native steerability (property change per unit steer) on the small model.\n3) ALIGNMENT LEARNING: On a shared held-out corpus of objects, collect paired residual-stream activations from source and target models (per-position, aligned by object/token). Learn a linear/orthogonal alignment map T (Procrustes / ridge regression, plus a rotation-invariant variant following the universality literature) between the two representation spaces. Report alignment quality (SVCCA, held-out reconstruction R^2). Compare same-family (size scaling) vs cross-architecture alignment difficulty.\n4) CORE TRANSFER TEST: Map each validated source direction into the target model via T and steer target generation. Metric: Transfer Efficiency = (target property gain from transferred direction) / (target property gain from a direction NATIVELY discovered+validated on the target). Also report absolute property gain, validity retention, novelty/diversity. Compare against baselines: (a) random-direction transfer through T (control), (b) naive same-index feature transfer without alignment, (c) no steering. Test across all size pairs, cross-architecture pairs, and the molecular pair.\n5) STEERABILITY-PROXY TEST (key practical claim): For a large bank of directions, correlate source-model steerability with (i) target-model steerability of the transferred direction and (ii) target-model steerability of the natively-rediscovered counterpart. Metric: Spearman correlation and precision@k of using the small model to pre-select which directions will steer the big model. Quantify compute saved = big-model generation runs avoided to find k good steering directions.\n6) MULTI-PROPERTY & FRONTIER: Transfer 2-3 directions jointly; measure joint constraint-satisfaction and interference on the target model to test whether transfer preserves compositionality.\n7) FAILURE ANALYSIS: Characterize where transfer breaks down (which property types, which layer pairs, cross-architecture vs within-family) and whether alignment quality predicts transfer efficiency.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Transfer Efficiency (primary), absolute property gain, validity retention (pLDDT/RDKit-validity/pseudo-perplexity), steerability-proxy Spearman + precision@k, compute saved, alignment quality (SVCCA/R^2), joint-constraint satisfaction, novelty/diversity.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Cross-architecture representation spaces (masked vs autoregressive; different tokenizers) may be too dissimilar for a linear alignment; we mitigate by also testing same-family size-scaling transfer (easier) and reporting where the linear assumption fails, which is itself informative. 2) In-silico oracles/validity are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal controllability and design compute-efficiency, not experimental viability. 3) Alignment may require a sizable paired-activation corpus; we quantify sensitivity to corpus size and use held-out objects to avoid leakage. 4) The DiMaS finding that features can be decodable-but-not-steerable means some directions may fail to transfer steerability even if the property signal transfers; we treat this as a measured outcome (the steerability-proxy test) rather than an assumption. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; we control with multiple layers and both SAE-feature and difference-of-means directions. 6) If transfer efficiency is low, the negative result still provides a valuable, publishable characterization of the limits of feature universality for controllable generation."
}}
