Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "causal_timing_window_design",
"Title": "The Right Feature at the Right Time: Causal Intervention Windows for Interpretable Control of Biomolecular Diffusion Models",
"Short Hypothesis": "During iterative diffusion generation of proteins and molecules, each interpretable feature has a distinct CAUSAL WINDOW\u2014a range of denoising timesteps where clamping it actually decides the corresponding property, outside of which the same intervention is inert or destroys validity. We hypothesize (i) property classes stratify by window (coarse/topological properties are decided early, fine/local properties late), and (ii) intervening only inside a feature's causal window achieves the target property with far higher validity and far fewer wasted intervention steps than the standard practice of applying steering uniformly across all timesteps or at a single pass.",
"Related Work": "Image-diffusion interpretability shows temporally-structured concept emergence: 'Emergence and Evolution of Interpretable Concepts in Diffusion Models' (2025) finds composition locks in early and style mid-process; TIDE (2025) builds temporal-aware SAEs; 'Unraveling the Temporal Dynamics of the Unet' (2023) finds composition/denoising phases. DLM-Scope (2026) does diffusion-time SAE steering for text but does not characterize property-specific causal windows. In the scientific-object space, all steering work (InterPLM 2024, ProtSAE 2025, ProtoMech 2026, antibody-SAE 2025, automated-neuron-labelling 2025) either uses masked/autoregressive models with single-pass interventions or applies steering uniformly, and none studies WHEN during a diffusion denoising trajectory an interpretable feature is causally decisive for a hard, verifiable property. The protein-DPO paper (2026) reports a training-time temporal decoupling of structure vs property, but concerns fine-tuning dynamics, not inference-time feature interventions. We are the first to (a) map property-class-specific causal intervention windows for interpretable features in biomolecular diffusion generation, (b) show that window-targeted intervention beats uniform/single-pass steering on the validity\u2013control frontier, and (c) turn timing into an efficiency lever (fewer intervention steps, cheaper compute) for scientific design.",
"Abstract": "Interpretability-guided design of proteins and molecules increasingly relies on clamping interpretable features to control target properties, but nearly all methods apply the intervention with the same strength at every step of the generative process. Work on image diffusion has shown that interpretable concepts emerge and lock in at different stages\u2014composition early, style later\u2014implying that WHEN you intervene matters as much as WHAT you intervene on. We ask whether biomolecular diffusion models exhibit analogous property-specific causal windows and whether exploiting them makes controllable scientific design more valid and efficient. Using discrete/continuous protein diffusion models and a SMILES/graph molecular diffusion model, we train sparse autoencoders on their per-timestep denoiser activations, identify features linked to hard, verifiable properties (secondary-structure content, fold topology, motifs for proteins; ring systems, functional groups, logP-band for molecules), and measure each feature's causal effect on the finished object when clamped only within a sliding timestep window. This yields a Causal Timing Map. We test two hypotheses: (1) property classes stratify by window\u2014coarse/topological properties are decided early, fine/local properties late; (2) window-targeted intervention Pareto-dominates uniform and single-pass steering on a control\u2013validity frontier while using far fewer intervention steps. We report timing maps, frontier comparisons, an efficiency accounting of intervention steps saved, and a simple scheduling recipe that assigns each desired property to its causal window. Results would establish timing as a first-class, efficiency-boosting control axis for interpretable scientific design.",
"Experiments": "1) MODELS & ORACLES: Use at least two iterative generators with accessible per-step denoiser activations: (a) a discrete/masked protein diffusion or order-agnostic protein LM (e.g., a masked-diffusion ESM-style or DPLM-type model) and (b) a molecular diffusion model over SMILES/graphs. Define target properties with HARD automated evaluators so 'the property in the finished object' is unambiguous: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), fold topology / contact-order proxy, GRAVY, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex, e.g., zinc finger); molecules\u2014ring count/aromatic-ring presence, specific functional groups (RDKit SMARTS), logP band, QED. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT and pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity and synthetic-accessibility for molecules.\n2) TEMPORAL SAEs: Train TopK SAEs on the denoiser residual-stream activations, either shared across timesteps or temporally-conditioned (TIDE-style), at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic. Verify reconstruction fidelity and per-timestep interpretability (concept-correlation of features with the hard properties, computed on held-out generations).\n3) CAUSAL TIMING MAP (core): For each property-linked feature, clamp it to a strong value only within a sliding window of timesteps (e.g., non-overlapping bins covering the trajectory, plus growing/shrinking windows), let generation finish, and measure the property change in the FINISHED object (via the hard evaluator) and the validity cost. Produce, per feature/property, a curve of causal effect and validity vs. window position/width\u2014the Causal Timing Map. Metric: window-localized causal effect (property gain per intervened step within window) and the minimal window achieving a target property gain.\n4) STRATIFICATION TEST (hypothesis 1): Classify each property as coarse/topological (fold class, overall structure content, scaffold ring system) vs fine/local (a single motif, one functional group). Test whether coarse properties have significantly earlier causal-window centers than fine properties (mixed-effects regression of window-center on property class; report effect size and CIs across both domains). Also test whether the window center correlates with when the feature's concept-correlation first becomes predictive of the final object during unsteered generation (linking causal timing to representational emergence).\n5) EFFICIENCY & FRONTIER TEST (hypothesis 2): Compare three intervention policies at matched target-property goals: (a) uniform steering across all timesteps (current practice), (b) single-pass/late-only steering, (c) window-targeted steering (intervene only inside each feature's mapped causal window). Metrics: control\u2013validity Pareto frontier (property success rate vs validity retention), number of intervention steps used (compute/efficiency), and novelty/diversity of outputs. Hypothesis-confirming result: window-targeted policy dominates the frontier while using far fewer intervention steps.\n6) MULTI-PROPERTY SCHEDULING: For 2\u20133 simultaneous properties whose causal windows differ, assign each to its own window (a timing schedule) and compare joint constraint-satisfaction + validity + interference against applying all interventions uniformly at all steps. Test whether temporal separation reduces interference between properties that collide when co-applied.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat reduced protocols across the two domains, two model checkpoints/sizes, and shared vs temporally-conditioned SAEs; report where the timing structure is stable vs model-specific.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Causal Timing Map (window-localized effect and minimal effective window), stratification effect size (early vs late window centers by property class), control\u2013validity frontier area, intervention-steps saved (efficiency), joint constraint-satisfaction, interference reduction from temporal scheduling, and SAE reconstruction/interpretability sanity metrics.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Requires generators with accessible per-step activations; if a target model's diffusion process is not easily instrumented we substitute alternatives, limiting generality to instrumentable models. 2) In-silico evaluators (ESMFold, DSSP, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal causal timing and controllability, not experimental viability. 3) Causal windows may be entangled with steering strength; we sweep strength within each window and report timing effects controlling for strength. 4) Temporally-shared SAEs may blur timestep-specific features; we mitigate with temporally-conditioned SAEs and report differences. 5) The stratification hypothesis could fail (some fine properties decided early); a null or reversed result is still an informative, publishable characterization. 6) Feature-property linkage relies on concept-correlation, which can be non-causal; the sliding-window clamp is itself the causal test, so we treat correlation only as a candidate-selection step. 7) Efficiency gains depend on how much of the trajectory the causal window occupies; if windows are broad, savings shrink\u2014this is directly quantified rather than assumed."
}}
