Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "internal_validity_foresight",
  "Title": "Failure Foresight: Do Interpretable Features Predict Invalid Scientific Generations Early Enough to Abort and Save Compute?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "During iterative/autoregressive generation of proteins and molecules, a small set of interpretable sparse-autoencoder features acts as an early internal 'validity alarm' that predicts, well before generation completes, whether the finished object will be invalid (unfoldable, chemically invalid, degenerate). Monitoring these features enables a cheap early-abort-and-restart policy that raises the yield of valid objects per unit compute far above running every generation to completion, and does so more efficiently and interpretably than output-probability or perplexity-based early stopping.",
  "Related Work": "A large body of interpretability work uses SAE features to detect and mitigate hallucinations in text and vision-language models (RAGLens 2025; SSL/SAVE/SAFE 2025; 'Do I Know This Entity?' 2024; H-Neurons 2025) and to detect hallucinations early from internal states in factual QA (Varshney et al. 2024; MHAD 2024; HaMI 2025; HD-NDEs 2025). Separately, interpretability-for-design work on proteins/molecules (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; ProtoMech 2026; molecular-LM SAEs) focuses on STEERING features to add properties, and evaluates validity only as a side constraint. No prior work (a) treats interpretable features as a predictive EARLY-WARNING signal for the objective, structurally-verifiable INVALIDITY of a to-be-generated scientific object, (b) uses that signal to drive an early-abort-and-restart policy that improves valid-yield-per-compute, or (c) compares such interpretable monitoring against output-probability/perplexity early-stopping baselines on a compute-efficiency frontier. We convert the 'model self-knowledge' insight from the text-hallucination literature into a concrete, compute-saving reliability tool for scientific design, where validity is an unambiguous physical/chemical fact rather than fuzzy factuality.",
  "Abstract": "Generative models for scientific design frequently emit invalid objects\u2014proteins that do not fold, molecules that violate chemistry, degenerate sequences\u2014and practitioners discover this only after paying the full cost of generation and validation. We ask whether a model's own interpretable internal features reveal, early in generation, that it is heading toward an invalid object, and whether acting on that signal can save substantial compute. Building on evidence from language models that sparse-autoencoder (SAE) features encode a form of self-knowledge, we train SAEs on the intermediate activations of protein and molecular generative models and search for a compact set of features whose early activations predict the eventual validity of the finished object, verified by hard structural evaluators (ESMFold pLDDT and pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit validity and synthetic-accessibility for molecules). We characterize how early and how accurately these 'validity-alarm' features forecast failure, and whether they are interpretable (e.g., firing on frustrated partial structures or chemically inconsistent fragments). We then deploy them in an early-abort-and-restart controller that terminates doomed generations and reallocates the saved budget, measuring valid objects produced per unit compute against three baselines: run-to-completion, output-probability/perplexity early stopping, and a black-box probe on raw activations. Our central claims are that (i) interpretable features predict eventual invalidity earlier and more accurately than output-side signals, and (ii) an interpretability-driven abort policy Pareto-dominates baselines on the valid-yield-versus-compute frontier while yielding human-readable failure diagnoses. Results would establish interpretable features as a practical reliability-and-efficiency layer for scientific generation.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS & VALIDITY LABELS: Use at least two generators with accessible intermediate activations: (a) a protein generator (autoregressive protein LM such as ProtGPT2/p-IgGen and/or a masked-diffusion protein LM), and (b) a molecular generator (a GPT-style SMILES LM and/or a graph/SMILES diffusion model). Define hard, automatic VALIDITY labels for finished objects: proteins\u2014ESMFold pLDDT above threshold AND pseudo-perplexity below threshold AND non-degenerate (low repeat/entropy); molecules\u2014RDKit parseable/valence-valid AND passes basic sanity (SA score, ring/atom-count bounds). Generate a large corpus (e.g., 20k) per model under normal sampling to obtain a balanced set of valid and invalid finished objects with their full generation trajectories (per-step activations saved).\n2) TRAIN SAEs & FIND VALIDITY-ALARM FEATURES: Train TopK SAEs on the generator's residual-stream activations at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic). For each generation step fraction (e.g., 10%,25%,50%,75%), compute SAE feature activations from the partial generation and measure each feature's predictive information (mutual information / AUROC) about eventual validity. Select a compact 'validity-alarm' feature set via information-based selection (as in RAGLens).\n3) EARLY-PREDICTION CHARACTERIZATION (core analysis): Plot forecast AUROC of the alarm features versus generation-progress fraction\u2014how early can invalidity be predicted? Compare against (i) output-side signals available at the same progress point: running token log-probability / partial pseudo-perplexity / predictive entropy, and (ii) a black-box linear/MLP probe on the raw (non-SAE) activations at the same layer/step. Metrics: AUROC and calibration at each progress fraction, and 'earliest step to reach AUROC=0.8'. Hypothesis: SAE-feature alarms reach reliable prediction earlier than output-side signals and are competitive with the black-box probe while remaining sparse and interpretable.\n4) INTERPRETABILITY OF ALARMS: Auto-label the top alarm features (max-activating partial generations; LLM- or annotation-based descriptions) and test faithfulness by ablating/clamping the feature and checking whether validity outcomes shift, and by correlating feature firing with human-recognizable failure precursors (e.g., low-complexity runs, sterically frustrated partial folds via partial-structure metrics, chemically inconsistent fragment prefixes for SMILES).\n5) EARLY-ABORT-AND-RESTART CONTROLLER (core application): Implement a controller that monitors the alarm-feature score during generation and, when it exceeds a threshold at a checkpoint, aborts and restarts (new seed/prompt) with the saved budget. Sweep the abort threshold to trace the valid-yield-per-compute frontier. Compute cost = total forward-pass/decoding steps (and ESMFold calls avoided). Baselines on the SAME frontier: (a) run-to-completion (no abort), (b) output-probability/perplexity-threshold early stopping, (c) black-box raw-activation probe abort, (d) random-abort control at matched abort rate. Primary metric: number of VALID objects produced per unit compute; secondary: diversity/novelty of the valid set (to ensure aborting does not collapse output distribution), and false-abort rate (valid generations wrongly killed).\n6) CONDITIONAL / STEERED SETTING: Repeat the frontier comparison when generation is CONDITIONED or STEERED toward a target property (a harder regime where invalidity is more frequent), testing whether alarm features generalize under distribution shift and remain useful precisely when validity is scarce.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Report across both domains, two model checkpoints/sizes, and two SAE variants; analyze where early prediction fails (which failure modes are unforeseeable).\nEVALUATION METRICS: forecast AUROC vs generation progress, earliest-reliable-prediction step, valid-yield-per-compute frontier area, false-abort rate, diversity/novelty retention, alarm-feature faithfulness (ablation effect), and interpretability labels.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Validity labels from ESMFold/RDKit are in-silico proxies for wet-lab viability; claims concern model-defined structural validity and generation efficiency, not experimental function. 2) Early prediction may be easy for gross degenerate failures but hard for subtle ones; we stratify results by failure type and report where foresight is impossible. 3) SAE-feature alarms might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, sparsity, interpretability, and compute-frontier gains constitute the contribution\u2014and we report this honestly. 4) Restart-based abort assumes generations are roughly i.i.d. in cost; for conditional generation restarts may correlate\u2014mitigated by seed/prompt diversification and reported. 5) Aborting could bias the output distribution (collapse diversity); we explicitly measure diversity/novelty retention. 6) Requires generators with accessible intermediate activations, limiting scope to instrumentable open models. 7) Threshold tuning could overfit; we select thresholds on a held-out split and report frontier robustness across seeds."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'internal_validity_foresight', 'Title': 'Failure Foresight: Do Interpretable Features Predict Invalid Scientific Generations Early Enough to Abort and Save Compute?', 'Short Hypothesis': "During iterative/autoregressive generation of proteins and molecules, a small set of interpretable sparse-autoencoder features acts as an early internal 'validity alarm' that predicts, well before generation completes, whether the finished object will be invalid (unfoldable, chemically invalid, degenerate). Monitoring these features enables a cheap early-abort-and-restart policy that raises the yield of valid objects per unit compute far above running every generation to completion, and does so more efficiently and interpretably than output-probability or perplexity-based early stopping.", 'Related Work': "A large body of interpretability work uses SAE features to detect and mitigate hallucinations in text and vision-language models (RAGLens 2025; SSL/SAVE/SAFE 2025; 'Do I Know This Entity?' 2024; H-Neurons 2025) and to detect hallucinations early from internal states in factual QA (Varshney et al. 2024; MHAD 2024; HaMI 2025; HD-NDEs 2025). Separately, interpretability-for-design work on proteins/molecules (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; ProtoMech 2026; molecular-LM SAEs) focuses on STEERING features to add properties, and evaluates validity only as a side constraint. No prior work (a) treats interpretable features as a predictive EARLY-WARNING signal for the objective, structurally-verifiable INVALIDITY of a to-be-generated scientific object, (b) uses that signal to drive an early-abort-and-restart policy that improves valid-yield-per-compute, or (c) compares such interpretable monitoring against output-probability/perplexity early-stopping baselines on a compute-efficiency frontier. We convert the 'model self-knowledge' insight from the text-hallucination literature into a concrete, compute-saving reliability tool for scientific design, where validity is an unambiguous physical/chemical fact rather than fuzzy factuality.", 'Abstract': "Generative models for scientific design frequently emit invalid objects—proteins that do not fold, molecules that violate chemistry, degenerate sequences—and practitioners discover this only after paying the full cost of generation and validation. We ask whether a model's own interpretable internal features reveal, early in generation, that it is heading toward an invalid object, and whether acting on that signal can save substantial compute. Building on evidence from language models that sparse-autoencoder (SAE) features encode a form of self-knowledge, we train SAEs on the intermediate activations of protein and molecular generative models and search for a compact set of features whose early activations predict the eventual validity of the finished object, verified by hard structural evaluators (ESMFold pLDDT and pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit validity and synthetic-accessibility for molecules). We characterize how early and how accurately these 'validity-alarm' features forecast failure, and whether they are interpretable (e.g., firing on frustrated partial structures or chemically inconsistent fragments). We then deploy them in an early-abort-and-restart controller that terminates doomed generations and reallocates the saved budget, measuring valid objects produced per unit compute against three baselines: run-to-completion, output-probability/perplexity early stopping, and a black-box probe on raw activations. Our central claims are that (i) interpretable features predict eventual invalidity earlier and more accurately than output-side signals, and (ii) an interpretability-driven abort policy Pareto-dominates baselines on the valid-yield-versus-compute frontier while yielding human-readable failure diagnoses. Results would establish interpretable features as a practical reliability-and-efficiency layer for scientific generation.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS & VALIDITY LABELS: Use at least two generators with accessible intermediate activations: (a) a protein generator (autoregressive protein LM such as ProtGPT2/p-IgGen and/or a masked-diffusion protein LM), and (b) a molecular generator (a GPT-style SMILES LM and/or a graph/SMILES diffusion model). Define hard, automatic VALIDITY labels for finished objects: proteins—ESMFold pLDDT above threshold AND pseudo-perplexity below threshold AND non-degenerate (low repeat/entropy); molecules—RDKit parseable/valence-valid AND passes basic sanity (SA score, ring/atom-count bounds). Generate a large corpus (e.g., 20k) per model under normal sampling to obtain a balanced set of valid and invalid finished objects with their full generation trajectories (per-step activations saved).\n2) TRAIN SAEs & FIND VALIDITY-ALARM FEATURES: Train TopK SAEs on the generator's residual-stream activations at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic). For each generation step fraction (e.g., 10%,25%,50%,75%), compute SAE feature activations from the partial generation and measure each feature's predictive information (mutual information / AUROC) about eventual validity. Select a compact 'validity-alarm' feature set via information-based selection (as in RAGLens).\n3) EARLY-PREDICTION CHARACTERIZATION (core analysis): Plot forecast AUROC of the alarm features versus generation-progress fraction—how early can invalidity be predicted? Compare against (i) output-side signals available at the same progress point: running token log-probability / partial pseudo-perplexity / predictive entropy, and (ii) a black-box linear/MLP probe on the raw (non-SAE) activations at the same layer/step. Metrics: AUROC and calibration at each progress fraction, and 'earliest step to reach AUROC=0.8'. Hypothesis: SAE-feature alarms reach reliable prediction earlier than output-side signals and are competitive with the black-box probe while remaining sparse and interpretable.\n4) INTERPRETABILITY OF ALARMS: Auto-label the top alarm features (max-activating partial generations; LLM- or annotation-based descriptions) and test faithfulness by ablating/clamping the feature and checking whether validity outcomes shift, and by correlating feature firing with human-recognizable failure precursors (e.g., low-complexity runs, sterically frustrated partial folds via partial-structure metrics, chemically inconsistent fragment prefixes for SMILES).\n5) EARLY-ABORT-AND-RESTART CONTROLLER (core application): Implement a controller that monitors the alarm-feature score during generation and, when it exceeds a threshold at a checkpoint, aborts and restarts (new seed/prompt) with the saved budget. Sweep the abort threshold to trace the valid-yield-per-compute frontier. Compute cost = total forward-pass/decoding steps (and ESMFold calls avoided). Baselines on the SAME frontier: (a) run-to-completion (no abort), (b) output-probability/perplexity-threshold early stopping, (c) black-box raw-activation probe abort, (d) random-abort control at matched abort rate. Primary metric: number of VALID objects produced per unit compute; secondary: diversity/novelty of the valid set (to ensure aborting does not collapse output distribution), and false-abort rate (valid generations wrongly killed).\n6) CONDITIONAL / STEERED SETTING: Repeat the frontier comparison when generation is CONDITIONED or STEERED toward a target property (a harder regime where invalidity is more frequent), testing whether alarm features generalize under distribution shift and remain useful precisely when validity is scarce.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Report across both domains, two model checkpoints/sizes, and two SAE variants; analyze where early prediction fails (which failure modes are unforeseeable).\nEVALUATION METRICS: forecast AUROC vs generation progress, earliest-reliable-prediction step, valid-yield-per-compute frontier area, false-abort rate, diversity/novelty retention, alarm-feature faithfulness (ablation effect), and interpretability labels.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) Validity labels from ESMFold/RDKit are in-silico proxies for wet-lab viability; claims concern model-defined structural validity and generation efficiency, not experimental function. 2) Early prediction may be easy for gross degenerate failures but hard for subtle ones; we stratify results by failure type and report where foresight is impossible. 3) SAE-feature alarms might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, sparsity, interpretability, and compute-frontier gains constitute the contribution—and we report this honestly. 4) Restart-based abort assumes generations are roughly i.i.d. in cost; for conditional generation restarts may correlate—mitigated by seed/prompt diversification and reported. 5) Aborting could bias the output distribution (collapse diversity); we explicitly measure diversity/novelty retention. 6) Requires generators with accessible intermediate activations, limiting scope to instrumentable open models. 7) Threshold tuning could overfit; we select thresholds on a held-out split and report frontier robustness across seeds.'}

