Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "shared_concept_causal_bridge", "Title": "One Knob, Two Worlds: Do Proteins and Molecules Share Causally-Steerable Physicochemical Concepts in a Unified Sparse Autoencoder?", "Short Hypothesis": "Abstract physicochemical concepts (hydrophobicity, charge, aromaticity, molecular size, polarity) are represented by SHARED interpretable directions in a jointly-trained cross-modality sparse autoencoder spanning a protein language model and a small-molecule language model, and\u2014critically\u2014steering a single shared feature causally and simultaneously controls the corresponding property in BOTH modalities' generations. We further hypothesize that shared features are MORE causally steerable and validity-preserving than modality-private features encoding the same concept, because they capture a modality-invariant physical abstraction.", "Related Work": "Two threads meet here but have never been combined for causal generative control across scientific object types. (1) Cross-model/cross-modal SAEs\u2014USAE (2025), SPARC (2025), and group-sparse multimodal SAEs (2026)\u2014learn shared concept dictionaries across vision/language models, and 'Beyond Converging Representations' (2025) shows scientific foundation models across string/graph/3D/protein modalities are representationally aligned and collapse when extrapolating. (2) Single-modality scientific SAE steering\u2014InterPLM (2024), the chemistry-LM SAE (2025), Matryoshka SAEs on ESM2-3B (2025), CB-pLM (2024)\u2014discovers and steers interpretable features WITHIN one model of one object type. Crucially: (a) all cross-modal SAE work is descriptive/retrieval-focused and never tests CAUSAL steering of generation; (b) all scientific steering work is confined to a single object type and never asks whether a concept has a single unified causal handle across proteins AND molecules. MolBind (2024) and protein-GDM alignment (2024) align modalities via contrastive learning but do not extract interpretable, individually-steerable, causally-validated shared features. We are the first to (i) train a unified SAE bridging a protein LM and a molecule LM, (ii) identify shared physicochemical concept features, and (iii) test whether ONE shared feature causally steers the SAME property in both modalities' generations\u2014turning cross-modal interpretability from a similarity metric into a shared design lever.", "Abstract": "Physicochemical concepts such as hydrophobicity, charge, aromaticity, and molecular size govern the behavior of both proteins and small molecules, yet generative models for each object type are studied in isolation and steered by modality-specific machinery. We ask a simple, elegant question: is there a single interpretable 'knob' that controls the same property across worlds? Building on recent cross-model sparse autoencoders (USAE, SPARC), we train a unified sparse autoencoder that jointly reconstructs the residual-stream activations of a protein language model (ESM-2) and a small-molecule SMILES language model, encouraging shared latent dimensions via a cross-reconstruction objective and global sparsity. We identify SHARED features\u2014dimensions that activate coherently in both modalities on a common physicochemical concept\u2014versus modality-private features. Our central test is causal: we clamp a single shared feature and measure whether it simultaneously and monotonically shifts the corresponding property in BOTH protein generations (GRAVY hydrophobicity, net charge, aromatic content via DSSP/biopython) and molecule generations (RDKit logP, formal charge, aromatic-ring count), while preserving validity (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity, synthetic-accessibility for molecules). We compare shared-feature steering against steering matched modality-private features and against difference-of-means directions per modality. We hypothesize shared features act as unified, physically-grounded causal handles that steer more reliably and with a better control-validity frontier than private ones. Success would establish modality-invariant interpretable design levers, letting a single validated concept-knob transfer intent across proteins and molecules and offering mechanistic evidence for a shared physical abstraction in scientific foundation models.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS & DATA: Protein side\u2014frozen ESM-2 (150M/650M); molecule side\u2014a frozen GPT-style SMILES chemistry LM (e.g., an FM4M/ChEMBL-trained CLM). Collect residual-stream activations at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic) for a large corpus of proteins (UniRef sample) and molecules (ChEMBL/ZINC sample). No paired protein-molecule data is needed; alignment is concept-level via the shared objective.\n2) UNIFIED SAE TRAINING: Train a joint TopK/global-TopK SAE that ingests activations from either modality and reconstructs them, following USAE/SPARC: a shared dictionary with modality-specific encoder/decoder heads and a cross-reconstruction term encouraging concept alignment. Baselines: two independent per-modality SAEs. Report reconstruction fidelity per modality and the fraction of latents that are cross-modally active (activate in both modalities), a measure of dictionary sharing.\n3) IDENTIFY SHARED CONCEPT FEATURES: For a target concept set {hydrophobicity, net charge, aromaticity, molecular size/weight, polarity}, define cheap automated property evaluators per modality (proteins: GRAVY, net charge at pH7, aromatic-residue fraction, molecular weight, polarity index via biopython; molecules: RDKit logP, formal/partial charge, aromatic-ring count, MW, TPSA). Score each SAE feature's concept-correlation in each modality. Label a feature SHARED for a concept if it has high concept-correlation in BOTH modalities; label matched modality-PRIVATE features with equally high correlation in only one modality. Report how many concepts admit shared features and the sharing rate versus the independent-SAE baseline (which cannot produce shared indices).\n4) CORE CAUSAL TEST\u2014ONE KNOB, TWO WORLDS: For each shared concept feature, clamp it to a swept range of values and GENERATE objects in each modality (masked infilling / activation-conditioned decoding for proteins; activation-steered autoregressive decoding for SMILES). Measure: (a) property change per unit steer in EACH modality (does one knob move both?), (b) sign/monotonicity consistency across modalities, (c) validity retention (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity; RDKit-validity, SA score). Primary metric: fraction of shared features that produce a significant, correctly-signed causal property shift in BOTH modalities (bidirectional causal success rate), with random-direction and unrelated-feature controls.\n5) SHARED VS PRIVATE COMPARISON: For each concept, compare the control-validity Pareto frontier of (i) the shared feature, (ii) the best matched modality-private feature, (iii) per-modality difference-of-means directions. Test the hypothesis that shared features achieve equal-or-better property gain at equal validity, and better cross-modal transfer of steering sign. Metrics: frontier area, max reliable property gain within validity budget, cross-modal sign-consistency.\n6) MECHANISTIC PROBE OF SHARING: Test whether a shared feature's steerability in one modality PREDICTS its steerability in the other (Spearman across features), which would support a genuinely unified handle rather than coincidental co-labeling. Also test the 'physical abstraction' claim by checking whether shared features generalize to a concept-varying held-out set better than private features.\n7) DESIGN-INTENT TRANSFER DEMO: Show a practical use\u2014validate a concept knob's strength once on the cheap molecule model, then apply the SAME shared feature to steer protein generation (and vice versa), reporting transfer efficiency (fraction of native effect recovered) as a compute-saving cross-modality design tool.\n8) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two protein model sizes, two SAE variants (global-TopK, group-sparse), and layer choices; report where sharing/steerability is stable versus artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: dictionary sharing rate; bidirectional causal success rate (primary); cross-modal steering sign-consistency; control-validity frontier area (shared vs private); cross-modal steerability Spearman; validity retention (pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, RDKit-validity, SA); transfer efficiency; reconstruction fidelity.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico property evaluators and validity metrics (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal, cross-modal controllability, not experimental viability. 2) The joint SAE may learn a 'split dictionary' where most features stay modality-private (the known failure mode from multimodal SAE work); we mitigate with global-TopK and group-sparse objectives and report sharing rate honestly\u2014a low sharing rate is itself an informative result about the limits of cross-modal abstraction. 3) A feature co-labeled as shared could reflect coincidental concept-correlation rather than a unified causal handle; the bidirectional causal test and cross-modal steerability-prediction analysis are designed to distinguish these. 4) Proteins and molecules have very different tokenizations and activation statistics, which may make joint reconstruction hard; we control by comparing to independent per-modality SAEs and reporting per-modality reconstruction fidelity. 5) Steering the molecule LM and masked/infilling protein generation are different decoding regimes; we standardize property measurement on finished objects and use random-direction controls to ensure comparability. 6) Layer/strength choices could confound sharing and steerability; we sweep strength within the validity budget and test multiple layers and SAE variants."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'shared_concept_causal_bridge', 'Title': 'One Knob, Two Worlds: Do Proteins and Molecules Share Causally-Steerable Physicochemical Concepts in a Unified Sparse Autoencoder?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Abstract physicochemical concepts (hydrophobicity, charge, aromaticity, molecular size, polarity) are represented by SHARED interpretable directions in a jointly-trained cross-modality sparse autoencoder spanning a protein language model and a small-molecule language model, and—critically—steering a single shared feature causally and simultaneously controls the corresponding property in BOTH modalities' generations. We further hypothesize that shared features are MORE causally steerable and validity-preserving than modality-private features encoding the same concept, because they capture a modality-invariant physical abstraction.", 'Related Work': "Two threads meet here but have never been combined for causal generative control across scientific object types. (1) Cross-model/cross-modal SAEs—USAE (2025), SPARC (2025), and group-sparse multimodal SAEs (2026)—learn shared concept dictionaries across vision/language models, and 'Beyond Converging Representations' (2025) shows scientific foundation models across string/graph/3D/protein modalities are representationally aligned and collapse when extrapolating. (2) Single-modality scientific SAE steering—InterPLM (2024), the chemistry-LM SAE (2025), Matryoshka SAEs on ESM2-3B (2025), CB-pLM (2024)—discovers and steers interpretable features WITHIN one model of one object type. Crucially: (a) all cross-modal SAE work is descriptive/retrieval-focused and never tests CAUSAL steering of generation; (b) all scientific steering work is confined to a single object type and never asks whether a concept has a single unified causal handle across proteins AND molecules. MolBind (2024) and protein-GDM alignment (2024) align modalities via contrastive learning but do not extract interpretable, individually-steerable, causally-validated shared features. We are the first to (i) train a unified SAE bridging a protein LM and a molecule LM, (ii) identify shared physicochemical concept features, and (iii) test whether ONE shared feature causally steers the SAME property in both modalities' generations—turning cross-modal interpretability from a similarity metric into a shared design lever.", 'Abstract': "Physicochemical concepts such as hydrophobicity, charge, aromaticity, and molecular size govern the behavior of both proteins and small molecules, yet generative models for each object type are studied in isolation and steered by modality-specific machinery. We ask a simple, elegant question: is there a single interpretable 'knob' that controls the same property across worlds? Building on recent cross-model sparse autoencoders (USAE, SPARC), we train a unified sparse autoencoder that jointly reconstructs the residual-stream activations of a protein language model (ESM-2) and a small-molecule SMILES language model, encouraging shared latent dimensions via a cross-reconstruction objective and global sparsity. We identify SHARED features—dimensions that activate coherently in both modalities on a common physicochemical concept—versus modality-private features. Our central test is causal: we clamp a single shared feature and measure whether it simultaneously and monotonically shifts the corresponding property in BOTH protein generations (GRAVY hydrophobicity, net charge, aromatic content via DSSP/biopython) and molecule generations (RDKit logP, formal charge, aromatic-ring count), while preserving validity (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity, synthetic-accessibility for molecules). We compare shared-feature steering against steering matched modality-private features and against difference-of-means directions per modality. We hypothesize shared features act as unified, physically-grounded causal handles that steer more reliably and with a better control-validity frontier than private ones. Success would establish modality-invariant interpretable design levers, letting a single validated concept-knob transfer intent across proteins and molecules and offering mechanistic evidence for a shared physical abstraction in scientific foundation models.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS & DATA: Protein side—frozen ESM-2 (150M/650M); molecule side—a frozen GPT-style SMILES chemistry LM (e.g., an FM4M/ChEMBL-trained CLM). Collect residual-stream activations at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic) for a large corpus of proteins (UniRef sample) and molecules (ChEMBL/ZINC sample). No paired protein-molecule data is needed; alignment is concept-level via the shared objective.\n2) UNIFIED SAE TRAINING: Train a joint TopK/global-TopK SAE that ingests activations from either modality and reconstructs them, following USAE/SPARC: a shared dictionary with modality-specific encoder/decoder heads and a cross-reconstruction term encouraging concept alignment. Baselines: two independent per-modality SAEs. Report reconstruction fidelity per modality and the fraction of latents that are cross-modally active (activate in both modalities), a measure of dictionary sharing.\n3) IDENTIFY SHARED CONCEPT FEATURES: For a target concept set {hydrophobicity, net charge, aromaticity, molecular size/weight, polarity}, define cheap automated property evaluators per modality (proteins: GRAVY, net charge at pH7, aromatic-residue fraction, molecular weight, polarity index via biopython; molecules: RDKit logP, formal/partial charge, aromatic-ring count, MW, TPSA). Score each SAE feature's concept-correlation in each modality. Label a feature SHARED for a concept if it has high concept-correlation in BOTH modalities; label matched modality-PRIVATE features with equally high correlation in only one modality. Report how many concepts admit shared features and the sharing rate versus the independent-SAE baseline (which cannot produce shared indices).\n4) CORE CAUSAL TEST—ONE KNOB, TWO WORLDS: For each shared concept feature, clamp it to a swept range of values and GENERATE objects in each modality (masked infilling / activation-conditioned decoding for proteins; activation-steered autoregressive decoding for SMILES). Measure: (a) property change per unit steer in EACH modality (does one knob move both?), (b) sign/monotonicity consistency across modalities, (c) validity retention (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity; RDKit-validity, SA score). Primary metric: fraction of shared features that produce a significant, correctly-signed causal property shift in BOTH modalities (bidirectional causal success rate), with random-direction and unrelated-feature controls.\n5) SHARED VS PRIVATE COMPARISON: For each concept, compare the control-validity Pareto frontier of (i) the shared feature, (ii) the best matched modality-private feature, (iii) per-modality difference-of-means directions. Test the hypothesis that shared features achieve equal-or-better property gain at equal validity, and better cross-modal transfer of steering sign. Metrics: frontier area, max reliable property gain within validity budget, cross-modal sign-consistency.\n6) MECHANISTIC PROBE OF SHARING: Test whether a shared feature's steerability in one modality PREDICTS its steerability in the other (Spearman across features), which would support a genuinely unified handle rather than coincidental co-labeling. Also test the 'physical abstraction' claim by checking whether shared features generalize to a concept-varying held-out set better than private features.\n7) DESIGN-INTENT TRANSFER DEMO: Show a practical use—validate a concept knob's strength once on the cheap molecule model, then apply the SAME shared feature to steer protein generation (and vice versa), reporting transfer efficiency (fraction of native effect recovered) as a compute-saving cross-modality design tool.\n8) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two protein model sizes, two SAE variants (global-TopK, group-sparse), and layer choices; report where sharing/steerability is stable versus artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: dictionary sharing rate; bidirectional causal success rate (primary); cross-modal steering sign-consistency; control-validity frontier area (shared vs private); cross-modal steerability Spearman; validity retention (pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, RDKit-validity, SA); transfer efficiency; reconstruction fidelity.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) In-silico property evaluators and validity metrics (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal, cross-modal controllability, not experimental viability. 2) The joint SAE may learn a 'split dictionary' where most features stay modality-private (the known failure mode from multimodal SAE work); we mitigate with global-TopK and group-sparse objectives and report sharing rate honestly—a low sharing rate is itself an informative result about the limits of cross-modal abstraction. 3) A feature co-labeled as shared could reflect coincidental concept-correlation rather than a unified causal handle; the bidirectional causal test and cross-modal steerability-prediction analysis are designed to distinguish these. 4) Proteins and molecules have very different tokenizations and activation statistics, which may make joint reconstruction hard; we control by comparing to independent per-modality SAEs and reporting per-modality reconstruction fidelity. 5) Steering the molecule LM and masked/infilling protein generation are different decoding regimes; we standardize property measurement on finished objects and use random-direction controls to ensure comparability. 6) Layer/strength choices could confound sharing and steerability; we sweep strength within the validity budget and test multiple layers and SAE variants."}
